teps and planned activities","exq.qs1.1.ok":false,"project.upload":"{\"name\":\"weekly report .docx\",\"type\":\"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document\",\"size\":16375,\"uploadedAt\":\"2026-08-17T17:38:41.750Z\",\"path\":\"fdc7dbcb-d492-4045-ba81-aaad6ef50f26/logbook/msxiphp2-weekly_report_.docx\"}","selfassess-0":true,"selfassess-1":true,"selfassess-2":true,"selfassess-3":true,"selfassess-4":true,"selfassess.savedAt":"2026-08-19T11:09:12.696Z","exq.qs1.0.tries":"1","exq.qs1.1.tries":"2","exq.qs1.2":"Distribute sensitive information through approved channels such as secure email, encrypted systems, password-protected documents, or authorised company platforms.\nAll sharing and handling of sensitive information must comply with company policies, data protection regulations, and confidentiality requirements. Security Measures\nOnly provide the information to employees or stakeholders who are authorised and require it to perform their duties, while maintaining confidentiality at all times.\nEnsure that the information is accurate, up to date, and obtained from authorised company sources before it is shared.\nUse Secure Distribution Methods\nDistribute sensitive information through approved channels such as secure email, encrypted systems, password-protected documents, or authorised company platforms.\nShare on a Need-to-Know Basis\nOnly provide the information to employees or stakeholders who are authorised and require it to perform their duties, while maintaining confidentiality at all times.","exq.qs1.2.ok":true,"exq.qs1.2.tries":"2","slidegate.0":true,"slidegate.1":true,"slidegate.2":true,"slidegate.3":true,"slidegate.4":true,"slidegate.5":true,"slidegate.6":true,"slidegate.7":true,"exq.qs1.1.reviewedText":"Presents the information collected, observations made, results obtained, or incidents recorded.\nProgress and Performance\nShows what has been completed, current status, achievements, and any targets that have been met.\nChallenges or Problems\nIdentifies issues, risks, delays, or obstacles affecting the work or project.\nRecommendations and Next Steps\nSuggests actions to be taken, solutions to problems, responsibilities, and future plans.\nDate and time of the incident\nDescription of the problem\nSystems or users affected\nCause of the incident (if known)\nActions taken to resolve it\nCurrent status (resolved or unresolved)\nRecommendations to prevent recurrence\nObjectives or tasks assigned\nTasks completed\nWork in progress\nChallenges encountered\nSkills learned or developed\nSupport required\nNext steps and planned activities","exq.qs1.1.marks":"4","exq.qs1.2.reviewedText":"Distribute sensitive information through approved channels such as secure email, encrypted systems, password-protected documents, or authorised company platforms.\nAll sharing and handling of sensitive information must comply with company policies, data protection regulations, and confidentiality requirements. Security Measures\nOnly provide the information to employees or stakeholders who are authorised and require it to perform their duties, while maintaining confidentiality at all times.\nEnsure that the information is accurate, up to date, and obtained from authorised company sources before it is shared.\nUse Secure Distribution Methods\nDistribute sensitive information through approved channels such as secure email, encrypted systems, password-protected documents, or authorised company platforms.\nShare on a Need-to-Know Basis\nOnly provide the information to employees or stakeholders who are authorised and require it to perform their duties, while maintaining confidentiality at all times.","exq.qs1.2.marks":"6","exq.qs1.0.reviewedText":"Progress or status reports â routine updates on work, projects or service performance.\nIncident or investigation reports â a factual record of what happened, why, and what was done.\nFeasibility reports / business cases â analysis and recommendation to support a decision.\nFinancial reports â budgets, expenditure, income and forecasts used to control money and plan spending.\nPerformance reports â monthly service or SLA reporting against targets, showing whether the service is meeting agreed levels.\nAudit and compliance reports â findings against standards, policies or regulations.\nFeasibility reports / business cases â analysis and recommendation to support a decision.","exq.qs1.0.marks":"12"},"quizzes":{},"exercises":{}},"10135":{"activities":{"Lesson & Training Aids":true,"Formative Assessment":true,"Summative Assessment":true,"POE Evidence":true},"quizzes":{"q-alignment":{"best":4,"total":4,"history":[{"date":"2026-07-24T08:25:57.229Z","score":4,"total":4},{"date":"2026-07-24T08:24:25.360Z","score":3,"total":4},{"date":"2026-07-24T08:22:55.344Z","score":1,"total":4}],"attempts":3},"q-so13":{"best":10,"total":10,"attempts":1,"history":[{"score":10,"total":10,"date":"2026-08-17T04:36:09.124Z"}]},"q-friday1":{"best":10,"total":10,"attempts":2,"history":[{"score":10,"total":10,"date":"2026-08-17T04:40:15.500Z"},{"score":9,"total":10,"date":"2026-08-17T04:38:57.663Z"}]},"q-friday2":{"best":10,"total":10,"attempts":2,"history":[{"score":10,"total":10,"date":"2026-08-17T04:46:09.499Z"},{"score":7,"total":10,"date":"2026-08-17T04:44:39.948Z"}]}},"logbook":{"exq.ex1.0":"Communicate clearly and regularly :  we need to share information amongst each other\nListen actively: Pay attention when you a member in the team is speaking\nBe reliable and accountable: Complete task on time\nSupport your teammates: always be willing to assist a member\nBe respectful: treat everyone professional and politely","exq.ex1.0.ok":false,"exq.ex1.1":"Don't Blame Others â People in your group lose respect for you if you're constantly blaming others for not meeting deadlines. You're not fooling anyone; people know who isn't pulling his weight in a group. Pointing the finger will only make you look cowardly. Group members understand if you have a heavy workload and weren't able to meet a deadline. Saying something like, \"I'm really sorry, but I'll get it to you by the end of today.\" will earn you a lot more respect than trying to make it seem like it's everyone else's fault that you missed your deadline.\nSupport Group Member's Ideas â If a team mate suggests something, always consider it â even if it's the silliest idea you've ever heard! Considering the group's ideas shows you're interested in other people's ideas, not just your own. And this makes you a good team member. After all, nobody likes a know-it-all.\nNo Bragging â It's one thing to rejoice in your successes with the group, but don't act like a superstar. Doing this will make others regret your personal successes and may create tension within the group. You don't have to brag to let people know you've done a good job, people will already know. Have faith that people will recognize when good work is being done and that they'll let you know how well you're doing. Your response? Something like \"Thanks, that means a lot.\" is enough.\nListen Actively â Look at the person who's speaking to you, nod, ask probing questions and acknowledge what's said by paraphrasing points that have been made. If you're unclear about something that's been said, ask for more information to clear up any confusion before moving on. Effective communication is a vital part of any team, so the value of good listening skills shouldn't be underestimated.","exq.ex1.1.ok":false,"exq.ex1.2":"Copilot said:\n1. Open Communication\nOpen communication means team members share information, ideas, concerns, and feedback honestly and clearly. Everyone feels comfortable expressing their opinions and asking questions without fear of criticism. This helps prevent misunderstandings, improves problem-solving, and ensures that all team members stay informed about important developments.\nExample: During a team meeting, members freely discuss project challenges and suggest solutions.\n2. Mutual Respect and Trust\nMutual respect and trust involve valuing each team member's contributions and believing that others will fulfill their responsibilities. Respect creates a positive working environment, while trust encourages cooperation and confidence among team members.\nExample: Team members listen attentively to each other's opinions and rely on one another to complete assigned tasks on time.\n3. Sharing Ideas and Responsibilities\nEffective teamwork requires members to contribute their knowledge, skills, and ideas while also sharing the workload fairly. This ensures that tasks are completed efficiently and that everyone feels involved in achieving team goals.\nExample: In a project, team members divide tasks according to their strengths and regularly share suggestions to improve outcomes.\n4. Supporting Team Members\nSupporting team members means helping colleagues when they face challenges, providing guidance when needed, and encouraging one another to succeed. A supportive team environment increases morale, productivity, and job satisfaction.\nExample: A team member assists a colleague who is struggling with a technical problem or a heavy workload.\n5. Collaboration and Constructive Feedback\nCollaboration involves working together to achieve a common goal, while constructive feedback helps team members improve their performance. Feedback should be specific, respectful, and focused on improvement rather than criticism","exq.ex1.2.ok":true,"exq.ex1.3":"Actively help the team or work unit accomplish its goals.\nAsk what are the team's specific goals and objectives; find out the team's milestones, dates and check-in times so the team can track progress toward goals.\nSuggest procedures or processes for achieving team goals. Help the team obtain resources as necessary.\nWhere possible, help clear away obstacles to the team's accomplishments.\nTake the time to help your fellow team mates, no matter the request.","exq.ex1.3.ok":true,"exq.ex1.4":"I will ensure all team members are consulted by:\nHolding regular team meetings and discussions.\nEncouraging everyone to share their opinions and ideas.\nPractising active listening and considering all viewpoints.\nAsking quieter members for their input.\nCreating an environment where everyone feels respected and valued.\nEnsuring decisions are discussed with the entire team before implementation.","exq.ex1.4.ok":true,"exq.ex1.5":"Most teams will have a leader â a very important position because he or she is responsible for the management of the entire project: setting a base agenda, facilitating meetings, and monitoring progress with communicating with members as needed.\nIt is important not to have too \"heavy\" a hand, or team morale may be lowered â all actions must be agreed to by the team.\nOther roles carry their own responsibilities: leader/editor, writer, graphic artist and Webmaster/HTML specialist â or initiator, recorder, devil's advocate/skeptic, optimist, timekeeper, gate keeper and summarizer.\nEveryone must agree on appropriate roles, and everyone must be satisfied in their roles","exq.ex1.5.ok":true,"exq.ex1.6":"The team leader will be responsible for coordinating and managing the project to ensure that objectives are achieved successfully\nAuthorities and responsibilities will only be assigned after team members agree on their roles and responsibilities\nEach team role carries specific responsibilities and decision-making authority within its area of expertise.\nAlthough the leader has management responsibilities, decisions should not be imposed on the team. Authority will be exercised through consultation and collaboration.","exq.ex1.6.ok":true,"exq.ex1.7":"Encourage open communication\nGive everyone an opportunity to share their ideas, concerns, and suggestions.\nCreate an environment where team members feel comfortable speaking up.\nActively listen to others\nPay attention to each person's viewpoint.\nAsk questions to clarify understanding and ensure everyone feels heard.\nRecognise individual strengths and needs\nUnderstand each team member's skills, experience, and preferred ways of working.\nAllocate tasks fairly based on these strengths.\nPromote inclusivity\nInvolve all team members in discussions and decision-making.\nRespect different backgrounds, perspectives, and opinions.\nSeek regular feedback\nCheck in with team members to identify any concerns or support they may need.\nAdjust team processes where necessary.","exq.ex1.7.ok":false,"exq.ex2.0":"Everyone has to put their agendas on the table for the others to examine â good teams uncover their own hidden agendas and expose them to the light of day.\nThe sooner we know one another's personal needs and hopes, the better for the team â acknowledging and addressing these needs as a group, early on, can help prevent \"selfish\" desires from sinking the team effort.\nIn team decisions and actions, actively seek the input of quiet team members, and ask what would make it easier for them to participate.\nAsk what are the team's specific goals and objectives â if there are none, work with other team members to create some.","exq.ex2.0.ok":true,"exq.ex2.2":"Come prepared for meetings.\nAttend meetings regularly.\nKeep deadlines.\nPublicly support the team.\nShow energy and enthusiasm.","exq.ex2.2.ok":true,"exq.ex2.1":"Foster a Positive Work Environment\nEncourage a positive attitude.\nPromote work-life balance.\nCreate a culture of support and encouragement.\nResolve Conflicts Quickly\nAddress disagreements promptly.\nEncourage constructive discussions.\nFocus on solutions rather than blame.\nEncourage Mutual Respect\nValue different opinions and backgrounds.\nTreat everyone fairly.\nPromote inclusiveness and equality.\nBuild Trust Among Team Members\nBe honest and transparent.\nKeep promises and commitments.\nEncourage reliability and accountability\nEstablish Clear Goals and Objectives\nEnsure all team members understand the team's purpose and objectives.\nSet achievable targets that everyone can work toward.\nAlign individual goals with team goa","exq.ex2.1.ok":false,"exq.ex2.8":"Promotes Clear Communication\nOpen discussion allows individuals to understand how their behaviour is affecting others and helps prevent misunderstandings.\nEncourages Trust and Respect\nWhen issues are discussed honestly and respectfully, team members feel valued and are more likely to trust one another.\nProvides an Opportunity for Improvement\nIndividuals may not realize that their behaviour is causing problems. Discussing it gives them a chance to correct their actions and improve.\nMaintains Team Morale and Performance\nUnresolved behavioural issues can lower morale, create tension, and reduce productivity. Prompt discussion helps maintain a positive working environment.","exq.ex2.8.ok":false,"exq.ex2.5":"Having different opinions is one of the essential benefits of teamwork â team members have strong feelings and emotions.\nLet each person state his or her view briefly, and have neutral team members reflect on areas of agreement or disagreement.\nAcknowledge that the conflict exists, gain common ground, and seek to understand all angles.\nDifferences can be identified through communication, observation, and interaction with team members. These differences should be acknowledged by respecting diverse opinions, backgrounds, experiences, and working styles while encouraging inclusion and equal participation.","exq.ex2.5.ok":true,"exq.ex2.3":"Don't Blame Others â people in your group lose respect for you if you're constantly blaming others for not meeting deadlines.\nNo Bragging â have faith that people will recognize when good work is being done and that they'll let you know how well you're doing.\nSupport group members' ideas and take the time to help your fellow team mates, no matter the request â if you've helped them in past, they'll be more than happy to lend a helping hand.\nUncover hidden agendas and expose them to the light of day â good teams recognise that this is how trust is built.","exq.ex2.3.ok":true,"exq.ex2.4":"Opinion vs fact\nExplain your reasons\nRestate and compliment ideas\nCritique the idea, not the person .\nCourteous, calm responses","exq.ex2.4.ok":true,"exq.ex2.7":"Look at the person who's speaking to you, nod, ask probing questions and acknowledge what's said by paraphrasing points that have been made.\nIf a team member asks you a question, answer it courteously; listen to others respectfully and fully.\nActively seek the input of quiet team members; recognize and use the differences and talents of others; critique the idea, not the person.\nTeam members can be treated with respect by listening to their opinions, valuing their contributions, communicating courteously, treating everyone fairly, recognising individual strengths, and respecting cultural and personal differences.","exq.ex2.7.ok":true,"exq.ex2.6":"Discourteous or disrespectful language is clearly detrimental to the functioning of the team.\nListen to others respectfully and fully â recognize and use the differences and talents of others.\nCritique the idea, not the person, and be courteous â morale may not be perfect all the time, but people should be able to work together.","exq.ex2.6.ok":false,"exq.ex4.0":"stakeholders are  Provincial Government of the Western Cape and its Provincial Tourism Safety and Security Unit, the City of Cape Town, the Tourism industry, Cape Town Routes Unlimited, the\nCape Town Tourism, the Provincial Government of the Western Cape and its Provincial Tourism Safety and Security Unit, the City of Cape Town, the Tourism industry and Cape Town Routes Unlimited.\nThe Cape Town Partnership, the Chambers, City Improvement Districts (CIDs), community representatives and the Safety and Security Forces (SAPS, emergency services and Ambulance & Fire).\nThe Tourism Victim Support programme, the Cape Town Tourism Members Safety Forum, the Cape Town Tourism-run Band Aid Programme, the media and the residents and visitors themselves.","exq.ex4.0.ok":true,"exq.ex4.1":"No single agency can be responsible for a safe destination, plan or react alone to visitor incidents â every network of service providers has a role to play, so a practical partnership approach is required.\nCo-ordination and communication is the single most important aspect in the Safety and Security Plan â in other words, an alignment that really works between all the parties.\nSolid relations ensure that once incidents are reported to the dedicated response mechanisms, the relevant Cape Town Tourism Manager can be contacted to ensure immediate activation of the Victim Support Programme.","exq.ex4.1.ok":true,"exq.ex4.2":"Effective communication with the Provincial Tourism Safety and Security Unit, the Tourism Victim Support programme, SAPS, emergency services, media and other key role players.\nCapacity to react quickly and effectively to visitor-related incidents, and immediate activation of the Victim Support Programme when incidents are reported.\nAwareness and communication campaigns, joint monitoring of emergency response times, identification of hotspots and quantifying the success of the plan.","exq.ex4.2.ok":true,"exq.ex4.3":"Awareness and communication campaigns, including distribution of practical safety tips to visitors and residents.\nProviding and assisting the media in obtaining correct and factual information about the Safety & Security Plan.\nEstablished and dedicated response mechanisms â telephone numbers such as 10111 for safety and security incidents, 10177 for Ambulance & Fire, 107 for other emergency services, and 0800 007 081 for consumer complaints.\nStructured forums for ongoing dialogue: the integrated Cape Town Safety Forum, Local Safety Forums throughout the Metropole, the Provincial and National safety forums, and the Cape Town Tourism Members Safety Forum","exq.ex4.3.ok":true,"exq.ex4.4":"By participating in and integrating with the Provincial and National safety forums, Local Safety Forums throughout the Metropole, and the Cape Town Tourism Members Safety Forum â established venues for open discussion.\nBy actively participating in and feeding into the Cape Town and Provincial Safety and Security Forums through the Cape Town Tourism Membership Forum.\nBy insisting on co-ordination and communication as the single most important aspect of the plan â an alignment that really works â so that every role player, from the citizen to the police, works together.\nBy ongoing, year-round proactive and reactive programmes, and by using dedicated response mechanisms so that everyone knows how to raise a concern and be heard.","exq.ex4.4.ok":true,"exq.ex4.5":"The image of Cape Town â and the tourism industry it depends on â cannot afford to be tarnished; broken commitments would damage the destination brand.\nThe city desperately relies on tourism as a vehicle to spread economic benefits to all its people and as a means of improving living standards by creating sustainable employment opportunities.\nHonouring commitments builds and maintains the trust between the stakeholders â Cape Town Tourism, government, SAPS, emergency services and community â that lets the partnership react quickly and effectively when incidents happen.\nIf commitments are not met, communication breaks down between the parties â and, as the introduction warns, a breakdown in communication leads directly to a breakdown in the organisation as a whole.","exq.ex4.5.ok":true,"project.upload":"{\"name\":\"weekly report on team members.docx\",\"type\":\"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document\",\"size\":16043,\"uploadedAt\":\"2026-08-17T17:48:18.082Z\",\"path\":\"fdc7dbcb-d492-4045-ba81-aaad6ef50f26/logbook/msxj1uea-weekly_report_on_team_members.docx\"}","selfassess-2":true,"selfassess-1":true,"selfassess-0":true,"selfassess-4":true,"selfassess-3":true,"selfassess.savedAt":"2026-08-19T11:10:13.710Z","exq.ex1.7.tries":"2","slidegate.21":true,"slidegate.22":true,"slidegate.23":true,"slidegate.24":true,"slidegate.25":true,"slidegate.26":true,"slidegate.27":true,"slidegate.28":true,"slidegate.29":true,"exq.ex1.0.tries":"2","exq.ex1.4.reviewedText":"I will ensure all team members are consulted by:\nHolding regular team meetings and discussions.\nEncouraging everyone to share their opinions and ideas.\nPractising active listening and considering all viewpoints.\nAsking quieter members for their input.\nCreating an environment where everyone feels respected and valued.\nEnsuring decisions are discussed with the entire team before implementation.","exq.ex1.4.marks":"8","exq.ex2.0.reviewedText":"Everyone has to put their agendas on the table for the others to examine â good teams uncover their own hidden agendas and expose them to the light of day.\nThe sooner we know one another's personal needs and hopes, the better for the team â acknowledging and addressing these needs as a group, early on, can help prevent \"selfish\" desires from sinking the team effort.\nIn team decisions and actions, actively seek the input of quiet team members, and ask what would make it easier for them to participate.\nAsk what are the team's specific goals and objectives â if there are none, work with other team members to create some.","exq.ex2.0.marks":"6","exq.ex4.2.reviewedText":"Effective communication with the Provincial Tourism Safety and Security Unit, the Tourism Victim Support programme, SAPS, emergency services, media and other key role players.\nCapacity to react quickly and effectively to visitor-related incidents, and immediate activation of the Victim Support Programme when incidents are reported.\nAwareness and communication campaigns, joint monitoring of emergency response times, identification of hotspots and quantifying the success of the plan.","exq.ex4.2.marks":"6","exq.ex1.5.reviewedText":"Most teams will have a leader â a very important position because he or she is responsible for the management of the entire project: setting a base agenda, facilitating meetings, and monitoring progress with communicating with members as needed.\nIt is important not to have too \"heavy\" a hand, or team morale may be lowered â all actions must be agreed to by the team.\nOther roles carry their own responsibilities: leader/editor, writer, graphic artist and Webmaster/HTML specialist â or initiator, recorder, devil's advocate/skeptic, optimist, timekeeper, gate keeper and summarizer.\nEveryone must agree on appropriate roles, and everyone must be satisfied in their roles","exq.ex1.5.marks":"4","exq.ex2.4.reviewedText":"Opinion vs fact\nExplain your reasons\nRestate and compliment ideas\nCritique the idea, not the person .\nCourteous, calm responses","exq.ex2.4.marks":"10","exq.ex2.5.reviewedText":"Having different opinions is one of the essential benefits of teamwork â team members have strong feelings and emotions.\nLet each person state his or her view briefly, and have neutral team members reflect on areas of agreement or disagreement.\nAcknowledge that the conflict exists, gain common ground, and seek to understand all angles.\nDifferences can be identified through communication, observation, and interaction with team members. These differences should be acknowledged by respecting diverse opinions, backgrounds, experiences, and working styles while encouraging inclusion and equal participation.","exq.ex2.5.marks":"6","exq.ex2.3.reviewedText":"Don't Blame Others â people in your group lose respect for you if you're constantly blaming others for not meeting deadlines.\nNo Bragging â have faith that people will recognize when good work is being done and that they'll let you know how well you're doing.\nSupport group members' ideas and take the time to help your fellow team mates, no matter the request â if you've helped them in past, they'll be more than happy to lend a helping hand.\nUncover hidden agendas and expose them to the light of day â good teams recognise that this is how trust is built.","exq.ex2.3.marks":"8","exq.ex4.1.reviewedText":"No single agency can be responsible for a safe destination, plan or react alone to visitor incidents â every network of service providers has a role to play, so a practical partnership approach is required.\nCo-ordination and communication is the single most important aspect in the Safety and Security Plan â in other words, an alignment that really works between all the parties.\nSolid relations ensure that once incidents are reported to the dedicated response mechanisms, the relevant Cape Town Tourism Manager can be contacted to ensure immediate activation of the Victim Support Programme.","exq.ex4.1.marks":"6","exq.ex4.0.reviewedText":"stakeholders are  Provincial Government of the Western Cape and its Provincial Tourism Safety and Security Unit, the City of Cape Town, the Tourism industry, Cape Town Routes Unlimited, the\nCape Town Tourism, the Provincial Government of the Western Cape and its Provincial Tourism Safety and Security Unit, the City of Cape Town, the Tourism industry and Cape Town Routes Unlimited.\nThe Cape Town Partnership, the Chambers, City Improvement Districts (CIDs), community representatives and the Safety and Security Forces (SAPS, emergency services and Ambulance & Fire).\nThe Tourism Victim Support programme, the Cape Town Tourism Members Safety Forum, the Cape Town Tourism-run Band Aid Programme, the media and the residents and visitors themselves.","exq.ex4.0.marks":"8","exq.ex4.3.reviewedText":"Awareness and communication campaigns, including distribution of practical safety tips to visitors and residents.\nProviding and assisting the media in obtaining correct and factual information about the Safety & Security Plan.\nEstablished and dedicated response mechanisms â telephone numbers such as 10111 for safety and security incidents, 10177 for Ambulance & Fire, 107 for other emergency services, and 0800 007 081 for consumer complaints.\nStructured forums for ongoing dialogue: the integrated Cape Town Safety Forum, Local Safety Forums throughout the Metropole, the Provincial and National safety forums, and the Cape Town Tourism Members Safety Forum","exq.ex4.3.marks":"6","exq.ex4.5.reviewedText":"The image of Cape Town â and the tourism industry it depends on â cannot afford to be tarnished; broken commitments would damage the destination brand.\nThe city desperately relies on tourism as a vehicle to spread economic benefits to all its people and as a means of improving living standards by creating sustainable employment opportunities.\nHonouring commitments builds and maintains the trust between the stakeholders â Cape Town Tourism, government, SAPS, emergency services and community â that lets the partnership react quickly and effectively when incidents happen.\nIf commitments are not met, communication breaks down between the parties â and, as the introduction warns, a breakdown in communication leads directly to a breakdown in the organisation as a whole.","exq.ex4.5.marks":"8","exq.ex4.4.reviewedText":"By participating in and integrating with the Provincial and National safety forums, Local Safety Forums throughout the Metropole, and the Cape Town Tourism Members Safety Forum â established venues for open discussion.\nBy actively participating in and feeding into the Cape Town and Provincial Safety and Security Forums through the Cape Town Tourism Membership Forum.\nBy insisting on co-ordination and communication as the single most important aspect of the plan â an alignment that really works â so that every role player, from the citizen to the police, works together.\nBy ongoing, year-round proactive and reactive programmes, and by using dedicated response mechanisms so that everyone knows how to raise a concern and be heard.","exq.ex4.4.marks":"8","exq.ex1.0.reviewedText":"Communicate clearly and regularly :  we need to share information amongst each other\nListen actively: Pay attention when you a member in the team is speaking\nBe reliable and accountable: Complete task on time\nSupport your teammates: always be willing to assist a member\nBe respectful: treat everyone professional and politely","exq.ex1.0.marks":"4","exq.ex1.1.reviewedText":"Don't Blame Others â People in your group lose respect for you if you're constantly blaming others for not meeting deadlines. You're not fooling anyone; people know who isn't pulling his weight in a group. Pointing the finger will only make you look cowardly. Group members understand if you have a heavy workload and weren't able to meet a deadline. Saying something like, \"I'm really sorry, but I'll get it to you by the end of today.\" will earn you a lot more respect than trying to make it seem like it's everyone else's fault that you missed your deadline.\nSupport Group Member's Ideas â If a team mate suggests something, always consider it â even if it's the silliest idea you've ever heard! Considering the group's ideas shows you're interested in other people's ideas, not just your own. And this makes you a good team member. After all, nobody likes a know-it-all.\nNo Bragging â It's one thing to rejoice in your successes with the group, but don't act like a superstar. Doing this will make others regret your personal successes and may create tension within the group. You don't have to brag to let people know you've done a good job, people will already know. Have faith that people will recognize when good work is being done and that they'll let you know how well you're doing. Your response? Something like \"Thanks, that means a lot.\" is enough.\nListen Actively â Look at the person who's speaking to you, nod, ask probing questions and acknowledge what's said by paraphrasing points that have been made. If you're unclear about something that's been said, ask for more information to clear up any confusion before moving on. Effective communication is a vital part of any team, so the value of good listening skills shouldn't be underestimated.","exq.ex1.1.marks":"0","exq.ex1.2.reviewedText":"Copilot said:\n1. Open Communication\nOpen communication means team members share information, ideas, concerns, and feedback honestly and clearly. Everyone feels comfortable expressing their opinions and asking questions without fear of criticism. This helps prevent misunderstandings, improves problem-solving, and ensures that all team members stay informed about important developments.\nExample: During a team meeting, members freely discuss project challenges and suggest solutions.\n2. Mutual Respect and Trust\nMutual respect and trust involve valuing each team member's contributions and believing that others will fulfill their responsibilities. Respect creates a positive working environment, while trust encourages cooperation and confidence among team members.\nExample: Team members listen attentively to each other's opinions and rely on one another to complete assigned tasks on time.\n3. Sharing Ideas and Responsibilities\nEffective teamwork requires members to contribute their knowledge, skills, and ideas while also sharing the workload fairly. This ensures that tasks are completed efficiently and that everyone feels involved in achieving team goals.\nExample: In a project, team members divide tasks according to their strengths and regularly share suggestions to improve outcomes.\n4. Supporting Team Members\nSupporting team members means helping colleagues when they face challenges, providing guidance when needed, and encouraging one another to succeed. A supportive team environment increases morale, productivity, and job satisfaction.\nExample: A team member assists a colleague who is struggling with a technical problem or a heavy workload.\n5. Collaboration and Constructive Feedback\nCollaboration involves working together to achieve a common goal, while constructive feedback helps team members improve their performance. Feedback should be specific, respectful, and focused on improvement rather than criticism","exq.ex1.2.marks":"0","exq.ex1.3.reviewedText":"Actively help the team or work unit accomplish its goals.\nAsk what are the team's specific goals and objectives; find out the team's milestones, dates and check-in times so the team can track progress toward goals.\nSuggest procedures or processes for achieving team goals. Help the team obtain resources as necessary.\nWhere possible, help clear away obstacles to the team's accomplishments.\nTake the time to help your fellow team mates, no matter the request.","exq.ex1.3.marks":"2","exq.ex1.6.reviewedText":"The team leader will be responsible for coordinating and managing the project to ensure that objectives are achieved successfully\nAuthorities and responsibilities will only be assigned after team members agree on their roles and responsibilities\nEach team role carries specific responsibilities and decision-making authority within its area of expertise.\nAlthough the leader has management responsibilities, decisions should not be imposed on the team. Authority will be exercised through consultation and collaboration.","exq.ex1.6.marks":"2","exq.ex1.7.reviewedText":"Encourage Open Communication\n\nGive every team member the opportunity to share their ideas, concerns, and feedback.\nUse meetings, discussions, and collaboration tools to ensure everyone has a voice.\nUnderstand Individual Strengths and Needs\n\nLearn each team member's skills, experience, and working style.\nAllocate tasks based on strengths while providing support where needed.\nPromote Inclusivity and Respect\n\nTreat all team members fairly and value different opinions and backgrounds.\nCreate an environment where everyone feels comfortable contributing\nSet Clear Goals and Expectations\n\nEnsure all team members understand the team's objectives, roles, and responsibilities.\nRegularly review progress and address any challenges together.\nProvide Continuous Feedback and Support\n\nEncourage constructive feedback between team members.\nOffer guidance, training, or resources to help everyone participate effectively and achieve team goals.","exq.ex1.7.marks":"0","exq.ex1.5.tries":"2","exq.ex1.4.tries":"2","exq.ex1.6.tries":"1","exq.ex1.3.tries":"1","exq.ex1.2.tries":"1","exq.ex1.1.tries":"1","exq.ex2.1.marks":"2","exq.ex2.2.marks":"8","exq.ex2.6.marks":"2","exq.ex2.7.marks":"4","exq.ex2.8.marks":"2","exq.ex2.1.reviewedText":"Foster a Positive Work Environment\nEncourage a positive attitude.\nPromote work-life balance.\nCreate a culture of support and encouragement.\nResolve Conflicts Quickly\nAddress disagreements promptly.\nEncourage constructive discussions.\nFocus on solutions rather than blame.\nEncourage Mutual Respect\nValue different opinions and backgrounds.\nTreat everyone fairly.\nPromote inclusiveness and equality.\nBuild Trust Among Team Members\nBe honest and transparent.\nKeep promises and commitments.\nEncourage reliability and accountability\nEstablish Clear Goals and Objectives\nEnsure all team members understand the team's purpose and objectives.\nSet achievable targets that everyone can work toward.\nAlign individual goals with team goa","exq.ex2.2.reviewedText":"Come prepared for meetings.\nAttend meetings regularly.\nKeep deadlines.\nPublicly support the team.\nShow energy and enthusiasm.","exq.ex2.6.reviewedText":"Discourteous or disrespectful language is clearly detrimental to the functioning of the team.\nListen to others respectfully and fully â recognize and use the differences and talents of others.\nCritique the idea, not the person, and be courteous â morale may not be perfect all the time, but people should be able to work together.","exq.ex2.7.reviewedText":"Look at the person who's speaking to you, nod, ask probing questions and acknowledge what's said by paraphrasing points that have been made.\nIf a team member asks you a question, answer it courteously; listen to others respectfully and fully.\nActively seek the input of quiet team members; recognize and use the differences and talents of others; critique the idea, not the person.\nTeam members can be treated with respect by listening to their opinions, valuing their contributions, communicating courteously, treating everyone fairly, recognising individual strengths, and respecting cultural and personal differences.","exq.ex2.8.reviewedText":"Promotes Clear Communication\nOpen discussion allows individuals to understand how their behaviour is affecting others and helps prevent misunderstandings.\nEncourages Trust and Respect\nWhen issues are discussed honestly and respectfully, team members feel valued and are more likely to trust one another.\nProvides an Opportunity for Improvement\nIndividuals may not realize that their behaviour is causing problems. Discussing it gives them a chance to correct their actions and improve.\nMaintains Team Morale and Performance\nUnresolved behavioural issues can lower morale, create tension, and reduce productivity. Prompt discussion helps maintain a positive working environment."},"exercises":{"ex2":{"best":70,"last":70,"total":82,"attempts":1},"ex4":{"best":64,"last":64,"total":66,"attempts":2}}},"114046":{"activities":{"Lesson & Training Aids":true,"Formative Assessment":true},"logbook":{"slidegate.0":true,"slidegate.1":true,"slidegate.2":true,"slidegate.3":true,"slidegate.4":true,"selfassess-3":true,"selfassess-2":true,"selfassess-1":true,"selfassess-0":true,"selfassess.savedAt":"2026-10-09T09:44:50.523Z","slidegate.5":true,"slidegate.6":true,"slidegate.7":true,"slidegate.8":true,"slidegate.9":true,"slidegate.10":true,"slidegate.11":true,"exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.0":"Types of network resources â Network resources include hardware like printers and servers, software applications, files, and data stored on the network that users can access and share.\nStorage types and access â Resources can be stored in primary memory, secondary storage like hard disks, or accessed via file systems that organize data into directories and files for easier management.\nNetwork services and devices â Managed resources also include network devices such as routers, switches, and firewalls, as well as services like email and internet access provided through the network.","exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.0.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.0.tries":"2","exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.1":"User\nHas basic access to the network and computer resources.\nCan use approved applications, access personal files, and shared resources.\nCannot change system settings or install software without permission.\nOperator\nHas more access than a regular user.\nCan perform routine tasks such as monitoring systems, running backups, and managing printers.\nCannot make major changes to the network or security settings.\nAdministrator\nHas the highest level of access and control.\nCan create user accounts, install software, configure systems, and manage security settings.\nResponsible for maintaining and securing the entire network.","exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.1.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.1.tries":"1","exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.2":". Personal Identification\nReason:\nEnsures that only authorized users can access the network and resources.\nProtects sensitive information from unauthorized access.\nMethod:\nUsernames and passwords\nBiometric authentication (fingerprints, facial recognition)\nIdentity cards or smart cards\nEmployees must enter a username and password to log in to the company network\nDirectory (Folder) Access\nReason:\nPrevents unauthorized users from viewing, modifying, or deleting shared folders.\nKeeps confidential information secure.\nMethod:\nAssign folder permissions such as Read, Write, or Full Control.\nRestrict access to specific users or groups.\nOnly the Finance department can access the Finance folder.\nFile Access\nReason:\nProtects important documents from unauthorized changes or deletion.\nEnsures data integrity and confidentiality.\nMethod:\nSet file permissions (Read, Write, Edit, Delete).\nUse encryption for sensitive files.\nEmployees can view a policy document but cannot edit it.\nTemporal Access (Time-Based Access)\nReason:\nLimits access to certain times to improve security and control resource usage.\nReduces the risk of unauthorized access outside working hours.\nMethod:\nConfigure login schedules.\nAllow access only during specified days and times.\nStaff can access the network only between 08:00 and 17:00.\nLocation Access\nReason:\nRestricts access based on where the user is connecting from.\nHelps prevent unauthorized access from unknown locations.\nMethod:\nAllow access only from approved offices or IP addresses.\nUse VPNs for remote access.\nEmployees can access company systems only from the office network or through a secure VPN.","exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.2.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.2.tries":"2","exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.3":"Monitor User Activity\nAn access audit trail records who accessed a system, file, or network resource and what actions they performed.\nIt helps organizations track user activities and identify unusual behavior.\nImprove Security and Accountability\nIt provides evidence of actions taken by users, making individuals accountable for their activities.\nIt helps detect unauthorized access, security breaches, or misuse of resources.\nSupport Investigations and Compliance\nAudit trails help administrators investigate incidents and determine what happened and when.\nThey also assist organizations in meeting legal, regulatory, and company policy requirements.","exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.3.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c.3.tries":"1","exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.0":"Speed of Devices\nFaster computers, servers, switches, and routers process data more quickly.\nDevices with slower processors or insufficient memory take longer to handle network requests.\nEffect: Faster devices reduce response time, while\nBandwidth of the Transmission Line\nBandwidth is the amount of data that can be transmitted per second.\nHigher bandwidth allows more data to travel at the same time.\nLower bandwidth can create congestion and delays.\nEffect: Higher bandwidth improves response time;\nQueuing at Nodes or Hosts\nNetwork devices may have to store incoming data in a queue when traffic is heavy.\nData packets wait their turn to be processed and forwarded.\nLonger queues result in longer delays.\nEffect: More queuing increases response time.\nProcessing Time\nEach device on the network must examine, process, and forward data packets.\nComplex operations such as encryption, filtering, or error checking require additional processing.\nEffect: Longer processing times increase response\nMessage Size and Overheads\nLarge messages take longer to transmit than small messages.\nNetwork protocols add extra information called overheads (headers, trailers, error-checking data).\nMore overhead means more data must be transmitted.\nEffect: Larger messages and greater overhead increase\nQuality of Transmission\nPoor-quality transmission lines can introduce errors due to noise or interference.\nCorrupted data must be retransmitted, causing delays.\nHigh-quality transmission reduces errors and retransmissions.\nEffect: Better transmission quality improves respond","exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.0.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.0.tries":"1","exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.1":"Improve Decision-Making\nAnalysing data helps organizations understand what is happening and make informed decisions based on facts rather than assumptions.\nIdentify Problems Early\nData analysis can reveal errors, trends, bottlenecks, or performance issues before they become major problems.\nIncrease Efficiency and Performance\nBy identifying problem areas, organizations can improve processes, reduce costs, save time, and increase productivity.","exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.1.ok":false,"exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.1.tries":"1","exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.2":"Identify Problems Quickly\nDiagnostic tools help detect faults, errors, or performance issues in a system, making troubleshooting faster and more effective.\nIdentify root causes â Tools help identify root causes of network issues by monitoring traffic, errors, and device performance.\nSupport Better Decision-Making\nThe data collected provides accurate information that can be used to find solutions, improve efficiency, and prevent future problems.Support Better Decision-Making","exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.2.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.3":"Faster Chips\nFaster processors (CPUs) in computers, servers, and network devices process data more quickly.\nThey reduce the time needed to execute instructions and handle network traffic.\nEffect on performance: Improves response times\nNetwork Boards\nUpgrading network interface cards (NICs) allows data to be sent and received faster.\nModern network boards support higher transmission speeds and better data handling.\nEffect on performance: Increases data transfer rat\n. Improved Protocols\nEfficient communication protocols reduce unnecessary data transmission and errors.\nThey improve how devices exchange and manage information.\nEffect on performance: Reduces delays, improves reliability, and increases network efficiency.\nHard Drive\nFaster hard drives, such as SSDs, access and store data more quickly than traditional HDDs.\nThis reduces the time needed to load applications and retrieve files.\nEffect on performance: Improves data access speed and overall system responsiveness\nMedia\nUsing higher-quality transmission media (such as fiber-optic cables instead of older copper cables) allows faster and more reliable communication.\nBetter media can support higher bandwidth and fewer transmission errors.\nEffect on performance: Increases transmission speed and reduces data loss.\nMemory\nIncreasing RAM allows more data and applications to be stored in fast-access memory.\nThis reduces the need to access slower storage devices.\nEffect on performance: Improves multitasking, processing speed, and response time.","exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.3.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.2.tries":"2","exq.manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b.3.tries":"1","exq.manual-activity-6026ddc0-7a48-4de9-9a7e-3619ba641c62.0":"Suppliers pros and cons â Suppliers offer lower installation costs and product guarantees but focus mainly on installation, leaving maintenance costs to the organisation.\nThird party professionals pros and cons â Third party professionals provide expert setup and maintenance but charge higher fees and have less direct product guarantee support, causing potential delays in repairs.\nLocal expertise pros and cons â Having an internal IT department reduces call-out costs and provides focused support but may lag in knowledge of new technologies until adopted by the organisation.","exq.manual-activity-6026ddc0-7a48-4de9-9a7e-3619ba641c62.0.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-6026ddc0-7a48-4de9-9a7e-3619ba641c62.0.tries":"1","exq.manual-activity-6026ddc0-7a48-4de9-9a7e-3619ba641c62.1":"User expectations from suppliers â Users expect suppliers to provide installation services at good rates and honour product guarantees promptly.\nUser expectations from third party professionals â Users expect expert network setup and maintenance but understand that product repairs may take longer due to indirect guarantee handling.\nUser expectations from local expertise â Users expect quick and cost-effective support from internal IT but may accept slower adoption of new technologies.\nUser expectations from product guarantees â Users expect that product guarantees will be honoured immediately by suppliers to cover faults or defects\nUser expectations from software tools â Users expect software tools to assist with troubleshooting and maintenance, improving network support efficiency.","exq.manual-activity-6026ddc0-7a48-4de9-9a7e-3619ba641c62.1.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-6026ddc0-7a48-4de9-9a7e-3619ba641c62.1.tries":"1","practical.pdf.final-assessor-signature":"__signature_removed__","practical.pdf.moderator-signature":"__signature_removed__","practical.pdf.assessor-signature":"__signature_removed__","practical.pdf.supervisor-signature":"__signature_removed__","learner-workbook.cover-name":"Pontsho","learner-workbook.cover-surname":"Molekane ","learner-workbook.cover-id":"0401290190081","learner-workbook.cover-contact":"0676920323","learner-workbook.name":"Pontsho Molekane ","learner-workbook.full-names":"Pontsho Molekane ","learner-workbook.learner-number":"0401290190081","learner-workbook.venue":"Investec ","learner-workbook.declaration-signature":"__saved_signature__","learner-workbook.declaration-name":"Pontsho Molekane","learner-workbook.facilitator-2":" Mr Snell","learner-workbook.learner-final-signature":"__saved_signature__","learner-workbook.self-learner-signature":"__saved_signature__","exq.manual-activity-5a13cf81-eb60-44ad-90d1-064365552ed0.0":"Trojan horse definition â A Trojan horse is a malicious program disguised as a useful or harmless file that installs harmful software when executed by the user.\nSymptoms of Trojan infection â Symptoms include unauthorized remote access, data corruption, spying, and interference with security software on the infected computer.\nTransmission methods â Trojans spread mainly by tricking users into running infected programs via email attachments, downloads, instant messages, or physical media.\nPrevention techniques â Prevent Trojans by avoiding opening unexpected attachments, not downloading unknown files, and using updated antivirus software.\nDetection and eradication â Detection involves scanning with antivirus tools and manual checks; eradication may require deleting files, registry edits, or system resets.\nSymptoms of Trojan infection â Symptoms include unauthorized remote access, data corruption, spying, and interference with security software on the infected computer.\nTransmission methods â Trojans spread mainly by tricking users into running infected programs via email attachments, downloads, instant messages, or physical media.\nPrevention techniques â Prevent Trojans by avoiding opening unexpected attachments, not downloading unknown files, and using updated antivirus software.\nDetection and eradication â Detection involves scanning with antivirus tools and manual checks; eradication may require deleting files, registry edits, or system resets.","exq.manual-activity-5a13cf81-eb60-44ad-90d1-064365552ed0.0.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-5a13cf81-eb60-44ad-90d1-064365552ed0.0.tries":"1","quiz.unit-quiz-c7a71582-621f-4e02-a1ca-5132ecbdf537.picks":"{\"0\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2],\"disp\":[0,2,3,1]},\"1\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1],\"disp\":[2,0,3,1]},\"2\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1],\"disp\":[3,2,1,0]},\"3\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1],\"disp\":[1,2,3,0]},\"4\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1],\"disp\":[2,1,0,3]},\"5\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2],\"disp\":[1,3,0,2]},\"6\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1],\"disp\":[0,1,3,2]},\"7\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0],\"disp\":[3,0,2,1]},\"8\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1],\"disp\":[0,3,2,1]},\"9\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1],\"disp\":[1,3,2,0]}}","learner-workbook.outcome-1.1.answer":"Network Interface Card (NIC)\n\nA hardware component installed in a computer or device that allows it to connect to the network.\n\nNetwork Cables\n\nPhysical cables, such as Ethernet (UTP) cables, used to transmit data between devices on the LAN.\n\nSwitch\n\nA networking device that connects multiple devices on the LAN and forwards data to the correct destination.\n\nRouter\n\nConnects the LAN to other networks, such as the Internet, and directs network traffic.\n\nWireless Access Point (WAP)\n\nProvides wireless connectivity for devices such as laptops, smartphones, and tablets.","learner-workbook.task-mark.1":"{\"awarded\":5,\"maxMarks\":5,\"feedback\":\"Good identification of key physical components of a LAN including NIC, cables, and network devices like switches and wireless access points. Router is more for connecting LAN to other networks but acceptable as a physical component related to LAN connectivity.\",\"incorrectSegments\":[]}","learner-workbook.outcome-1.2.answer":"Network Access Control (NAC) is a security system that controls and manages who or what devices can access a network. It checks users and devices before allowing them to connect and ensures they comply with the organization's security policies.\n\nKey Functions of NAC\nAuthentication â Verifies the identity of users and devices.\nAuthorization â Determines what resources users or devices can access.\nPolicy Enforcement â Ensures devices meet security requirements, such as having updated antivirus software.\nAccess Restriction â Blocks or limits access for unauthorized or non-compliant devices.","learner-workbook.task-mark.2":"{\"awarded\":5,\"maxMarks\":5,\"feedback\":\"Clear and accurate definition of NAC including its role in controlling network access based on security policies. The explanation of authentication, authorization, policy enforcement, and access restriction aligns well with the memo.\",\"incorrectSegments\":[]}","learner-workbook.outcome-1.3.answer":"An audit trail is a chronological record of activities and events in a computer system. It logs information such as who accessed a system, what actions were performed, when they occurred, and sometimes where the access originated from.\n\nBenefits of Audit Trails\n\nImproves Security\n\nHelps detect unauthorized access, suspicious activities, and security breaches.\n\nSupports Accountability\n\nRecords user actions, making individuals responsible for their activities.\n\nAssists Troubleshooting\n\nProvides information that can be used to identify and resolve system problems.\n\nEnsures Compliance\n\nHelps organizations meet legal, regulatory, and company policy requirements.\nObjectives of Audit Trails\n\nMonitor User Activity\n\nTrack who accessed systems, files, and resources.\n\nDetect and Investigate Incidents\n\nIdentify security violations, errors, or misuse of resources.\n\nProtect Data Integrity\n\nVerify that information has not been altered without authorization.\n\nProvide Evidence\n\nSupply records for audits, investigations, and compliance reviews.","learner-workbook.task-mark.3":"{\"awarded\":5,\"maxMarks\":5,\"feedback\":\"Comprehensive definition of audit trails and well outlined benefits and objectives matching the memo. Includes key points such as recording user activities, improving security, accountability, troubleshooting, compliance, and evidence provision.\",\"incorrectSegments\":[]}","learner-workbook.outcome-2.4.answer":"LAN performance issues occur when the network does not operate efficiently, resulting in slow communication, delays, or reduced productivity.\n\n1. Insufficient Bandwidth\nWhen too many users or applications share the same network connection, the available bandwidth becomes congested.\nThis can cause slow file transfers, delayed access to resources, and poor application performance.\n2. Network Congestion\nHeavy network traffic can overwhelm network devices such as switches and routers.\nData packets may be delayed or dropped, leading to slower response times.\n3. Faulty Network Hardware\nDamaged cables, faulty network interface cards (NICs), malfunctioning switches, or routers can reduce network performance.\nHardware failures may cause intermittent connectivity and packet loss.\n4. High Latency\nLatency is the time it takes for data to travel across the network.\nHigh latency causes delays in applications such as video conferencing, online meetings, and remote access.\n5. Packet Loss\nNetwork packets may be lost due to congestion, faulty hardware, or interference.\nLost packets must be retransmitted, which slows down communication.","learner-workbook.task-mark.4":"{\"awarded\":5,\"maxMarks\":5,\"feedback\":\"Good explanation of LAN performance issues including bandwidth congestion, network congestion, hardware faults, latency, and packet loss. These align well with the memo's points about transmission errors, hardware/software failures, and evolving LAN needs.\",\"incorrectSegments\":[]}","practical.pdf.final-learner-signature":"__saved_signature__","practical.pdf.learner-signature":"__saved_signature__","learner-workbook.telephone":"0676920323","learner-workbook.outcome-3.5.answer":"LAN support issues are problems that affect the performance, connectivity, and availability of devices and services on a Local Area Network.\n\n1. Connection Problems\nDevices cannot connect to the network.\nLoose or damaged network cables.\nFaulty network ports or switches.\n2. Slow Network Performance\nNetwork congestion caused by too many users.\nInsufficient bandwidth.\nLarge file transfers consuming network resources.\n3. Hardware Failures\nFailed switches, routers, NICs, or servers.\nPower outages affecting network equipment.\nOverheating network devices.\n4. Configuration Errors\nIncorrect IP addresses.\nDuplicate IP addresses on the network.\nMisconfigured DNS or DHCP settings.\n5. Security Issues\nUnauthorized access to the network.\nMalware and virus infections.\nWeak passwords and poor access control.","learner-workbook.task-mark.5":"{\"awarded\":5,\"maxMarks\":5,\"feedback\":\"Thorough explanation of LAN support issues covering connection problems, slow performance, hardware failures, configuration errors, and security issues. These correspond well with the memo's detailed points on LAN support challenges.\",\"incorrectSegments\":[]}","learner-workbook.outcome-4.6.answer":"A computer virus is malicious software that can infect computers and spread across a network, causing damage to files, slowing systems, or stealing information.\nFile Infector Virus\nAttaches itself to executable files (.exe, .com).\nSpreads when infected files are shared across the network.\nCan corrupt or delete files and programs.\nWorm\nA worm is a self-replicating malware that spreads automatically through network connections.\nDoes not require user action to spread.\nCan consume network bandwidth and slow down the LAN.\nMacro Virus\nInfects documents such as Microsoft Word and Microsoft Excel files.\nSpreads when users share infected documents through email or network folders\nTrojan Horse\nDisguises itself as legitimate software.\nOnce installed, it may steal data, allow unauthorized access, or download other malware\nRansomware\nEncrypts files and demands payment to restore access.\nCan spread across shared network drives and affect multiple computers","learner-workbook.task-mark.6":"{\"awarded\":5,\"maxMarks\":5,\"feedback\":\"Excellent explanation of typical viruses on LANs including file infector viruses, worms, macro viruses, trojans, and ransomware. The answer covers key virus types and their behaviors consistent with the memo.\",\"incorrectSegments\":[]}","learner-workbook.mark.outcome-1":"{\"awarded\":15,\"maxMarks\":15,\"feedback\":\"3 of 3 questions fully correct.\"}","learner-workbook.mark.outcome-2":"{\"awarded\":5,\"maxMarks\":5,\"feedback\":\"1 of 1 questions fully correct.\"}","learner-workbook.mark.outcome-3":"{\"awarded\":5,\"maxMarks\":5,\"feedback\":\"1 of 1 questions fully correct.\"}","learner-workbook.mark.outcome-4":"{\"awarded\":5,\"maxMarks\":5,\"feedback\":\"1 of 1 questions fully correct.\"}","learner-workbook.marked-at":"2026-10-09T05:35:13.522Z","exq.manual-activity-5a13cf81-eb60-44ad-90d1-064365552ed0.1":"","exq.manual-activity-5a13cf81-eb60-44ad-90d1-064365552ed0.1.ok":false,"exq.manual-activity-5a13cf81-eb60-44ad-90d1-064365552ed0.1.tries":"","slidegate.12":true,"slidegate.13":true,"slidegate.14":true,"slidegate.15":true,"slidegate.16":true,"slidegate.17":true,"slidegate.18":true,"slidegate.19":true,"slidegate.20":true,"slidegate.21":true,"slidegate.22":true,"slidegate.23":true},"exercises":{"manual-activity-7fde574d-46c8-4c5e-aa8b-c6e93011e93c":{"best":28,"last":28,"total":28,"attempts":1},"manual-activity-5ddb79c1-37d7-4328-ae72-5dd4872fd04b":{"best":34,"last":34,"total":36,"attempts":1},"manual-activity-6026ddc0-7a48-4de9-9a7e-3619ba641c62":{"best":14,"last":14,"total":16,"attempts":2},"manual-activity-5a13cf81-eb60-44ad-90d1-064365552ed0":{"best":6,"last":6,"total":20,"attempts":1}},"quizzes":{"unit-quiz-c7a71582-621f-4e02-a1ca-5132ecbdf537":{"best":10,"total":10,"attempts":3,"history":[{"score":10,"total":10,"date":"2026-10-09T05:19:03.690Z"},{"score":9,"total":10,"date":"2026-10-09T05:17:53.936Z"},{"score":7,"total":10,"date":"2026-10-09T05:15:26.190Z"}]}}},"114050":{"activities":{"Formative Assessment":true,"Lesson & Training Aids":true,"Summative Assessment":true,"POE Evidence":true},"logbook":{"exq.qs114050b.1":"A partnership is a business association between people who intend making and sharing profits.\nIt has a minimum of two and a maximum of 20 partners and is not a legal person.\nPartners are jointly and severally liable for the partnership's debts.","exq.qs114050b.1.ok":true,"exq.qs114050b.0":"A sole trader is owned by one person â there are no partners or co-owners.\nNo formal registration, administration or termination is needed and no statutes regulate sole owners.\nIf the business becomes insolvent, the owner personally becomes insolvent.","exq.qs114050b.0.ok":true,"exq.qs114050b.2":"It improves the business's ability to acquire capital.\nA limited company is registered under the Companies Act (Act 61 of 1973) and is a legal person separate from its owners.\nIt eliminates unlimited liability â the shareholders' liability is limited.","exq.qs114050b.2.ok":true,"exq.qs114050b.3":"A private company is owned by one or more private individuals, founders, or investors.\nShares are owned privately and are not offered to the general public.\nEffect:\nOwners have greater control over business decisions.\nLimited Liability\nDefinition:\nThe owners' personal assets are protected if the company incurs debts or losses.\nShareholders are only liable up to the amount they have invested in the company.\nEffect:\nReduces personal financial risk for the owners.","exq.qs114050b.3.ok":true,"exq.qs114050b.4":"A public company has at least seven members (shareholders) and at least two directors.\nIts shares may be offered to the general public and are freely transferable.\nIts name ends with Ltd and it is subject to numerous legal requirements and limitations.","exq.qs114050b.4.ok":true,"exq.qs114050b.5":"Buying and selling is the core trading activity â the business buys goods or services from suppliers and sells them to its clients.\nThe business interacts with clients by offering goods or services at prices clients are prepared to pay.","exq.qs114050b.5.ok":true,"exq.qs114050b.6":"Businesses charge clients more for goods or services than they cost, so profit is made from serving clients.\nSome businesses aim for profit maximisation while others are satisfied with enough profit to keep the owners comfortable.","exq.qs114050b.6.ok":true,"exq.qs114050b.7":".Charities interact with their clients to raise funds and provide help â their aims and objectives are led by the beliefs they stand for, not by profit.","exq.qs114050b.7.ok":true,"exq.qs114050sys.0":"A system is a group of interconnected parts that work together as a whole to achieve a specific goal or purpose. A system is also organised in an orderly arrangement according to a plan, where each part has a specific function and contributes to the operation of the entire system.","exq.qs114050sys.0.ok":false,"exq.qs114050sys.1":"Input â the system takes in resources such as labour, money, materials, equipment or data from its environment.\nProcess â the system converts or transforms the inputs into something useful.\nOutput â the system delivers the useful products, goods, services or information to its customers.","exq.qs114050sys.1.ok":true,"exq.qs114050sys.2":"Transaction Processing System (TPS) is a type of information system that is used to gather, record, manipulate and retrieves data related to the day-to-day business transactions of an organisation. A Transaction Processing System (TPS) collects, stores, modifies and retrieves the transactions of an organisation. A transaction is an event that generates or modifies data that is eventually stored in an information system. To be considered a transaction processing system, the computer must pass the ACID test. Contrasted with batch processing: batch processing is not transaction processing. Batch processing involves processing several transactions at the same time, and the results of each transaction are not immediately available when the transaction is being entered. Features of transaction processing systems: 1Rapid response â fast performance with a rapid response time is critical. Businesses cannot afford to have customers waiting: the turnaround from input of the transaction to production of the output must be a few seconds or less.2Reliability â many organisations rely heavily on their TPS; a breakdown will disrupt operations or even stop the business. The failure rate must be very low, with quick and accurate recovery and well-designed backup and recovery procedures.3Inflexibility â a TPS wants every transaction processed in the same way regardless of the user, the customer or the time of day. If a TPS were flexible there would be too many opportunities for non-standard operations â an airline must consistently accept reservations from every travel agency.4Controlled processing â the processing must support the organisation's operations: if roles and responsibilities are allocated to particular employees, the TPS should force and maintain this requirement.","exq.qs114050sys.2.ok":false,"exq.qs114050sys.4":"A Management Information System converts data from internal and external sources into information managers use to make effective decisions for planning, directing and controlling. It serves decisions at the operational, tactical and strategic levels of the organisation.","exq.qs114050sys.4.ok":true,"exq.qs114050sys.3":"A Knowledge Work System helps people deal with problems requiring technical expertise or knowledge. Its software includes word processing, spreadsheets, database management systems, CAD, project management and expert systems.","exq.qs114050sys.3.ok":true,"exq.qs114050b.8":"A social club interacts with its clients by providing social, recreational, or community activities where members can meet, participate in events, and enjoy shared interests. The club focuses on serving its members and creating social benefits rather than making a profit.   9","exq.qs114050b.8.ok":true,"exq.qs114050b.9":"Production and Service Delivery\nBusiness Activity:\nBusinesses produce goods or provide services to meet customer needs.\nExamples include manufacturing products, providing IT support, banking services, and retail sales.\nBusiness Environment:\nBusinesses operate in markets where customer demand, competition, and technology influence how products and services are delivered.\nMarketing and Sales\nBusiness Activity:\nBusinesses promote and sell their products or services to customers.\nThis includes advertising, market research, customer service, and sales activities.\nBusiness Environment:\nMarketing is affected by customer preferences, competitors, economic conditions, and market trends.\nFinance and Administration\nBusiness Activity:\nBusinesses manage money, budgets, payroll, purchasing, and records to ensure smooth operations.\nBusiness Environment:\nFinancial activities are influenced by government regulations, taxes, interest rates, and the overall economy.","exq.qs114050b.9.ok":false,"exq.qs114050apps.0":"Creating and Editing Business Documents\nFunction:\nWord processors are used to create, edit, and format documents such as letters, reports, meeting minutes, proposals, and contracts.\nEffect on the Business:\nProduces professional-looking documents.\nImproves communication with clients and staff.\nSaves time when making changes or updates to documents.\nStoring and Managing Information\nFunction:\nDocuments can be saved electronically, organized into folders, and easily searched when needed.\nEffect on the Business:\nReduces paper usage and storage costs.\nMakes information easy to retrieve.\nImproves record keeping and document management.\nImproving Productivity and Accuracy\nFunction:\nWord processors provide tools such as spell check, grammar check, templates, and mail merge.\nEffect on the Business:\nReduces errors in business documents.\nSpeeds up document creation.\nIncreases employee productivity and efficiency.","exq.qs114050apps.0.ok":true,"exq.qs114050apps.0.tries":"1","exq.qs114050apps.1":"A spreadsheet holds numbers in a grid of rows, columns and cells, and formulas recalculate automatically whenever a value changes.\nBusinesses use spreadsheets â Excel, Google Sheets â for budgets, cash-flow forecasts, price lists, sales analysis, charts and âwhat ifâ scenarios.\nEffect: calculations are accurate and instant, scenarios are tested before money is spent, and managers make better-informed decisions.","exq.qs114050apps.1.ok":true,"exq.qs114050apps.1.tries":"2","exq.qs114050apps.3":"Graphics software digitally creates and edits visual content â images, illustrations, animation and video.\nBusinesses use tools like Canva, Photoshop and Figma for logos and branding, adverts, social-media posts, product photos, packaging and presentations.\nEffect: a professional visual image builds customer trust, and producing artwork in-house is faster and cheaper than outsourcing design.","exq.qs114050apps.3.ok":true,"exq.qs114050apps.3.tries":"1","exq.qs114050apps.4":"An integrated office suite bundles the word processor, spreadsheet, presentations and email into one package with a shared look, and the applications work together â an Excel chart linked into a Word report updates when the numbers change\nSuites such as Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace add cloud storage, meetings and AI assistants, so files are co-authored and available on any device.\nEffect: one subscription covers most office work, data flows between applications instead of being retyped, and skills learned in one application transfer to the others.","exq.qs114050apps.4.ok":true,"exq.qs114050apps.4.tries":"1","exq.qs114050apps.2":"Store and Organize Business Information\nFunction:\nDatabases store large amounts of information such as customer records, employee details, inventory, sales, and financial data.\nData can be easily updated, searched, and retrieved.\nEffect on Business:\nImproves efficiency and reduces paperwork.\nEnsures information is accurate and readily available for decision-making.\nSupport Business Operations\nFunction:\nDatabases help manage daily activities such as order processing, stock control, payroll, and customer relationship management (CRM).\nMultiple users can access and update data simultaneously.\nEffect on Business:\nSpeeds up business processes.\nImproves customer service and increases productivity\n. Assist Decision-Making and Reporting\nFunction:\nDatabases generate reports, analyze trends, and provide business insights.\nManagers can monitor performance using up-to-date information.\nEffect on Business:\nEnables better planning and informed decision-making.\nHelps identify opportunities, reduce costs, and improve profitability.","exq.qs114050apps.2.ok":true,"exq.qs114050apps.2.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.0":"A business collects raw facts from its daily operations â sales, purchases, stock counts, customer details, times and amounts.\nData is raw and has no meaning of itself; it is captured and stored (for example in spreadsheets and databases) so it can later be processed.","exq.qs114050data.0.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.0.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.1":"Data is given meaning by relational connection â relating one fact to another, as a relational database does with its tables.\nProcessing data (sorting, grouping, comparing, summarising) turns it into information that answers who, what, where and when.","exq.qs114050data.1.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.1.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.2":"information is communicated to decision makers, who select a course of action among variations.\nDecisions can be structured and rational or intuitive under time pressure â either way the information must reach the right people to produce the final choice.","exq.qs114050data.2.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.2.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.3":"Prices are signals set by supply and demand, and an organisation's prices are largely governed by what competitors charge for similar products.\nPricing, costing and manufacturing departments communicate the information so goods sell at the same or better price than competitors.","exq.qs114050data.3.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.3.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.4":"Quality information ensures products and services meet or exceed customer requirements â fit for purpose, done right the first time.\nQuality assurance covers design through documentation, uses PDCA (PlanâDoâCheckâAct), and measures the organisation's quality against competitors","exq.qs114050data.4.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.4.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.5":"Marketing uses information from meetings between R&D and sales & marketing so new products are fully understood and marketed to consumers.\nIt is two-way: client surveys tell the business what customers want, and that information goes back to R&D to build it.","exq.qs114050data.5.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.5.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.6":"Business performance management organises, automates and analyses the metrics, processes and systems that drive performance â the next generation of business intelligence.\nPerformance information shows which tools work, so they can be applied where the business performs less well â using financial, human and material resources efficiently.","exq.qs114050data.6.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.6.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.8":"Viruses and malware corrupt, steal or destroy data â today above all ransomware arriving through phishing email.\nDefences include antivirus, updates and patches, backups, and staff who think before they click.","exq.qs114050data.8.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.10":"HR needs employment information: employee records, contracts, leave and attendance, payslips and performance reviews.","exq.qs114050data.10.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.13":"Finance needs cash-flow information: invoices, payments, debtors and creditors, cash-flow forecasts and budgets.","exq.qs114050data.13.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.13.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.10.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.8.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.7":"Unauthorized access can lead to data theft and loss of confidentiality.\nUnauthorized access can result in data being modified or deleted, affecting business operations.","exq.qs114050data.7.ok":false,"exq.qs114050data.7.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.9":"Defences include least-privilege access, audit trails of who did what, and removing access immediately when someone leaves.\nDisgruntled staff are insiders who deliberately damage, leak or delete the organisation's data.\nDefences include least-privilege access, audit trails of who did what, and removing access immediately when someone leaves.","exq.qs114050data.9.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.9.tries":"2","exq.qs114050data.11":"Production Planning Information\nInformation Needed:\nQuantity of products to be manufactured.\nProduction schedules, raw material requirements, and work orders.\nPurpose/Effect:\nEnsures the right products are produced at the right time.\nHelps avoid production delays and shortages.","exq.qs114050data.11.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.11.tries":"1","exq.qs114050data.12":"Customer and Market Information\nInformation Needed:\nCustomer needs and preferences.\nMarket trends, competitor activities, and target market data.\nPurpose/Effect:\nHelps the business understand its customers.\nSupports the development of effective marketing and branding strategies.\n2. Sales and Advertising Information\nInformation Needed:\nSales figures, campaign results, and advertising performance.\nCustomer feedback and brand awareness data.\nPurpose/Effect:\nMeasures the success of marketing campaigns.\nHelps improve branding, increase sales, and attract new customers.","exq.qs114050data.12.ok":true,"exq.qs114050data.12.tries":"2","exq.qs114050b.1.tries":"1","exq.qs114050b.2.tries":"1","exq.qs114050b.3.tries":"2","exq.qs114050b.9.tries":"2","slidegate.0":true,"slidegate.1":true,"slidegate.2":true,"slidegate.3":true,"slidegate.4":true,"slidegate.5":true,"slidegate.6":true,"slidegate.7":true,"slidegate.8":true,"slidegate.9":true,"slidegate.10":true,"slidegate.11":true,"slidegate.12":true,"slidegate.13":true,"slidegate.14":true,"slidegate.15":true,"slidegate.16":true,"slidegate.17":true,"slidegate.18":true},"exercises":{"qs114050b":{"best":36,"last":36,"total":48,"attempts":2},"qs114050sys":{"best":24,"last":24,"total":24,"attempts":1},"qs114050apps":{"best":22,"last":22,"total":30,"attempts":1},"qs114050data":{"best":44,"last":44,"total":48,"attempts":1}},"quizzes":{}},"114051":{"activities":{"Lesson & Training Aids":true,"Formative Assessment":true,"Summative Assessment":true,"POE Evidence":true},"logbook":{"exq.qs114051a.4":"Explain why the note taker at a technical practitioners meeting should have a technical background.Accurate Understanding of Technical Discussions\nTechnical meetings often include specialisted terms, acronyms, and concepts. A note taker with technical knowledge can understand the discussion correctly and record accurate information.\nAbility to Capture Important Details\nTechnical decisions, system requirements, risks, and action items can be complex. A technically skilled note taker can identify key points and distinguish important information from general conversation.\nReduced Errors and Misinterpretation\nWithout a technical background, the note taker may misunderstand technical terminology or record information incorrectly. A technical understanding helps ensure that meeting minutes are reliable and useful for future reference.\nBetter Follow-Up on Action Items\nTechnical knowledge enables the note taker to clearly document tasks, responsibilities, and deadlines, making it easier for team members to follow up on agreed actions.\nImproved Communication with the Team\nA technically knowledgeable note taker can communicate effectively with practitioners, ask charitying questions when necessary, and ensure that the meeting record reflects the discussion accurately.","exq.qs114051a.4.ok":true,"exq.qs114051a.0":"1.Contract meetings deal with negotiating, reviewing and managing contracts with clients and suppliers.\nTechnical review meetings evaluate designs, systems and technical work against requirements and standards.\nProject review meetings track project progress, risks, resources and milestones.","exq.qs114051a.0.ok":true,"exq.qs114051a.1":"In a democratic meeting the members make the decisions through discussion and voting â the chair guides the process but the group decides.\nIn an autocratic meeting the chairperson controls the meeting and takes the decisions, with members giving input only when asked.","exq.qs114051a.1.ok":true,"exq.qs114051a.2":"A facilitative meeting is led by a facilitator who guides the discussion rather than making decisions for the group.\nA facilitator guides the process of the meeting by managing participation, keeping discussions on track, and helping the group follow the agenda.\nThe meeting focuses on helping the group work together, solve problems, reach consensus, and achieve agreed objectives effectively.","exq.qs114051a.2.ok":false,"exq.qs114051a.3":"Voting\neach member votes and the option with the majority is adopted.\nConsensus discussion continues until every member can accept the decision\nCriteria based rating options are scored against agreed criteria and the highest score wins.\nRanking   members place the options in order of preference and the rankings are combined.\nPaired comparisons   options are compared two at a time and the overall preferred option is selected","exq.qs114051a.3.ok":true,"exq.qs114051b.0":"","exq.qs114051b.0.ok":false,"exq.qs114051b.2":"Timeous invitations give participants enough time to prepare â to study the agenda and read the documentation.\nParticipants can arrange their schedules and diaries so they are available for the meeting.\nThe right people actually attend because their attendance was secured well in advance.","exq.qs114051b.2.ok":true,"quiz.q-meeting-types.picks":"{\"0\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"1\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"2\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"3\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"4\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"5\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]}}","quiz.q-decisions-conventions.picks":"{\"0\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"1\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"2\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"3\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"4\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"5\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]}}","quiz.q-prep-chair.picks":"{\"0\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"1\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"2\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"3\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"4\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"5\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]}}","quiz.q-followup.picks":"{\"0\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"1\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"2\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"3\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"4\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]}}","selfassess-0":true,"selfassess-1":true,"selfassess-2":true,"selfassess-3":true,"selfassess-6":true,"selfassess-5":true,"selfassess-4":true,"selfassess.savedAt":"2026-09-04T11:58:14.608Z","exq.qs114051a.3.tries":"1","exq.qs114051b.1":".Clear, concise, documented outcomes keep the meeting focused on its purpose â participants know what must be achieved\nThe agenda can be planned and structured around the documented outcomes.\nAfter the meeting, success can be measured and evaluated against the documented outcomes.","exq.qs114051b.1.ok":true,"exq.qs114051b.1.tries":"1","exq.qs114051b.2.tries":"1","exq.qs114051b.3":"Meeting Agenda\n\nA list of topics to be discussed during the meeting.\nHelps participants prepare and understand the purpose of the meeting.\nNotice of Meeting / Invitation\n\nIncludes the date, time, venue (or online link), and purpose of the meeting.\nEnsures all participants know when and where the meeting will take place.\nRelevant Documents and Previous Minutes\n\nSupporting documents, reports, and minutes from the previous meeting.\nAllows participants to review information beforehand and contribute effectively.","exq.qs114051b.3.ok":false,"exq.qs114051c.0":"Promotes Order and Discipline\n\nRules help ensure that everyone follows a structured process and avoids disruptions.\nEncourages Fair Participation\n\nGuidelines give all members an equal opportunity to speak, share ideas, and contribute to discussions.\nImproves Meeting Effectiveness\n\nAgreed rules help the meeting stay focused on the agenda and achieve its objectives within the allocated time.","exq.qs114051c.0.ok":false,"exq.qs114051c.1":"The chairperson works through the agenda item by item.\nThe chairperson keeps discussion on the current item and parks unrelated issues.\nThe chairperson watches the time allocated to each agenda item","exq.qs114051c.1.ok":true,"exq.qs114051c.1.tries":"1","exq.qs114051c.2":"Invite quiet members by name to give input and use round-robin turns so everyone speaks.\nAcknowledge each contribution and keep debate on the issue, not the person.\nSummarise areas of agreement before moving on to the next point.","exq.qs114051c.2.ok":true,"exq.qs114051c.2.tries":"1","exq.qs114051c.4":"Every decision must have a timeframe or deadline for its implementation.\nThe person responsible must be recorded and the decision confirmed with the members before the meeting moves on.\n.Every decision taken in the meeting must be clear, accurate and unambiguous so nobody misunderstands what was decided.","exq.qs114051c.4.ok":true,"exq.qs114051c.3":"Urgent and important topics are prioritised and placed early on the agenda.\nEach topic gets a time allocation in proportion to its importance.\nLess critical items are moved to the end of the agenda, deferred or carried over to the next meeting.","exq.qs114051c.3.ok":true,"exq.qs114051c.3.tries":"2","exq.qs114051c.4.tries":"1","exq.qs114051d.0":"Minutes must capture each discussion, decision and action with its owner and timeframe.\nMinutes must follow the organisation's minute format and template as required by organisational policy.\nMinutes must be checked and distributed within the required time.","exq.qs114051d.0.ok":false,"exq.qs114051d.0.tries":"","exq.qs114051d.1":"","exq.qs114051d.1.ok":false,"exq.qs114051d.1.tries":"","exq.qs114051d.2":"","exq.qs114051d.2.ok":false,"exq.qs114051d.2.tries":"","slidegate.7":true,"slidegate.8":true,"slidegate.9":true,"slidegate.0":true,"slidegate.1":true,"slidegate.2":true,"slidegate.3":true,"slidegate.4":true,"slidegate.5":true,"slidegate.6":true,"slidegate.10":true,"slidegate.11":true,"slidegate.12":true,"slidegate.13":true,"slidegate.14":true,"slidegate.15":true,"slidegate.16":true,"slidegate.17":true,"slidegate.18":true,"slidegate.19":true,"slidegate.20":true,"slidegate.21":true,"slidegate.22":true,"slidegate.23":true,"slidegate.24":true,"slidegate.25":true,"slidegate.26":true,"slidegate.27":true},"quizzes":{"q-meeting-types":{"best":6,"total":6,"attempts":2,"history":[{"score":6,"total":6,"date":"2026-09-04T11:52:28.503Z"},{"score":4,"total":6,"date":"2026-09-04T11:50:31.184Z"}]},"q-decisions-conventions":{"best":6,"total":6,"attempts":2,"history":[{"score":6,"total":6,"date":"2026-09-04T11:54:50.429Z"},{"score":5,"total":6,"date":"2026-09-04T11:53:36.331Z"}]},"q-prep-chair":{"best":6,"total":6,"attempts":1,"history":[{"score":6,"total":6,"date":"2026-09-04T11:56:29.691Z"}]},"q-followup":{"best":5,"total":5,"attempts":1,"history":[{"score":5,"total":5,"date":"2026-09-04T11:57:43.374Z"}]}},"exercises":{"qs114051a":{"best":26,"last":26,"total":32,"attempts":1},"qs114051b":{"best":16,"last":16,"total":26,"attempts":1},"qs114051c":{"best":24,"last":24,"total":30,"attempts":1},"qs114051d":{"best":4,"last":4,"total":22,"attempts":1}}},"114055":{"activities":{"Formative Assessment":true,"Lesson & Training Aids":true,"Summative Assessment":true,"POE Evidence":true},"logbook":{"exq.ex1.2":"BITF (Black IT Forum) represents the interests of black students, professionals, and businesses in the ICT sector. Its focus is on transformation, skills development, mentorship, economic inclusion, and increasing participation of black professionals in the South African ICT industry.","exq.ex1.2.ok":true,"exq.ex1.3":"ITUC â The International Trade Union Confederation is the world's largest trade union federation, formed on 1 November 2006 out of the merger of the International Confederation of Free Trade Unions (ICFTU) and the World Confederation of Labour (WCL). It assists the South African IT market in trading internationally, empowering the market â especially its emerging sector.\n ","exq.ex1.3.ok":true,"exq.ex1.1":"The CSSA (Computer Society of South Africa) is one of, if not the most respected associations concerned with South African Information Technology.\nIt is established to elevate ICT capability and professionalism in South Africa: facilitating the exchange of opinions and views on ICT, representing industry practitioners to inform and lobby Government on ICT policy, disseminating ICT information via journals, lectures, seminars and conferences, improving technical knowledge and elevating the professional status of persons engaged in ICT.\nIt publishes the Code of Practice and Code of Conduct that prescribe minimum standards of practice and behaviour for all professional members.","exq.ex1.1.ok":true,"exq.ex1.4":"The ITA (Information Technology Association) is the official trade and employer body of the ICTe industry, striving to promote consistent standards of professionalism and service.\nIts purpose is to represent and promote the interests of its members â employers active in the Information Technology sector â nationally and internationally at governmental, NGO, parastatal and business level.\nFounded in 1934, it was renamed the ITA after the amalgamation of the Business Equipment Association and the Computer Services Association.\nIt assembles and disseminates information, provides a networking and marketing platform, and operates through three divisions: the Information Technology Users Council (ITUC), the Recruitment Consultancy Services Group (ITARCS) and the Payroll Authors Group (PAG).","exq.ex1.4.ok":true,"exq.ex1.0":"Security, Privacy and Confidentiality Security, privacy, and confidentiality are important professional practices in the computer industry. ccountability and Responsibility IT professionals must be accountable and responsible for their actions and decisions. Unacceptable Practices Some practices are considered unprofessional and unethical in the computer industry, including: False competence: Claiming to have skills or knowledge that you do not possess.Software piracy: Using, copying, or distributing software without proper licensing or authorization.Neglect: Failing to perform assigned duties, ignoring security procedures, or not maintaining systems properly.Unauthorized access: Accessing systems, files, or data without permission.Falsifying records: Providing false information in reports, documentation, or support tickets.       ","exq.ex1.0.ok":true,"exq.ex1.5":"Set out by the CSSA Code of Practice Minimum standards observed in the spirit Security, development, implementation and live systems Compliance with laws: Follow all relevant legislation, regulations, and company policies.","exq.ex1.5.ok":true,"exq.ex2.1":"Copyright Laws\nSoftware developers protect their programs through copyright laws.\nPeople who copy or distribute software illegally can face legal action, fines, or even imprisonment.\n2. Software Licensing\nMost software is sold with a license agreement that specifies how it may be used.\nUsers must purchase a valid license before installing or using the software legally.\n3. Product Activation and License Keys\nSoftware companies use activation codes, serial numbers, and product keys to prevent unauthorized use.\nThe software may stop working if the license is not genuine.\n4. Anti-Piracy Technology\nCompanies develop security features such as encryption, digital rights management (DRM), and online verification systems.\nThese technologies make it harder to copy or distribute software illegally.\n5. Awareness Campaigns\nThe computer industry educates users about the dangers of software piracy.\nPirated software can contain viruses, malware, and security vulnerabilities.","exq.ex2.1.ok":true,"exq.ex2.0":"Fair Recruitment and Selection Companies recruit employees based on their skills, qualifications, and experience rather than personal characteristics. This gives everyone an equal chance to apply for jobs and advance their careers. 2. Equal Access to Training and Development Employees are given opportunities to attend training courses, workshops, and certification programs to improve their skills and progress in their careers. 3. Non-Discrimination The computer industry promotes workplaces where individuals are treated fairly and respectfully. Discrimination based on race, gender, disability, age, religion, or culture is not acceptable.","exq.ex2.0.ok":true,"exq.ex2.2":"Legislation: the Intellectual Property Laws Amendment Act (effective 1 October 1997) strengthened protection for computer programs, compilations of data and databases, moving South Africa closer to compliance with the World Trade Organisation's TRIPS agreement.\nEnforcement and prosecution: the Business Software Alliance recovers losses through out-of-court settlements and prosecutes offenders; law enforcement (SAPS) and SAFACT conduct crackdowns and raids on pirated DVDs and CDs.\nIndustry and marketplace action: online marketplaces such as bidorbuy work with SAPS, SAFACT, the BSA and ICASA to combat pirated goods sold online; and public awareness campaigns remind South Africans that buying, selling or illegally copying software supports crime.","exq.ex2.2.ok":true,"selfassess-0":true,"selfassess-1":true,"selfassess-2":true,"selfassess.savedAt":"2026-08-14T12:49:20.082Z","project.upload":"{\"name\":\"Report on an Ideal Code of Ethics in the South African IT Industry.docx\",\"type\":\"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document\",\"size\":16252,\"uploadedAt\":\"2026-08-17T17:53:53.812Z\",\"path\":\"fdc7dbcb-d492-4045-ba81-aaad6ef50f26/logbook/msxj91g4-Report_on_an_Ideal_Code_of_Ethics_in_the_South_African_IT_Industry.docx\"}"},"exercises":{"ex1":{"best":46,"last":46,"total":48,"attempts":3},"ex2":{"best":20,"last":20,"total":24,"attempts":2}},"quiz":{"best":15,"total":15,"attempts":3,"history":[{"score":15,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-17T05:01:49.629Z"},{"score":14,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-17T04:57:27.230Z"},{"score":13,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-14T12:09:01.571Z"}]}},"114059":{"activities":{"Lesson & Training Aids":true,"Formative Assessment":true,"Summative Assessment":true,"POE Evidence":true},"logbook":{"exq.manual-activity-61e25cd8-8548-4af8-a898-1bd559abddb6.0":"Purpose of cost/benefit analysis â Cost/benefit analysis helps decide whether a proposed change or project is worth implementing by comparing its total expected costs against its total expected benefits.\nComponents of cost/benefit analysis â The analysis includes identifying all costs (one-off and ongoing) and benefits (often received over time), then calculating metrics like payback period to assess when benefits repay costs.\nSubjectivity in estimating benefits â Estimating benefits can involve subjective judgments, especially when including intangible factors like risks or reputation, which must be assigned monetary values\nFormal and informal uses â Cost/benefit analysis can be a formal project appraisal tool or an informal decision-making approach, both involving weighing costs against benefits to choose the best option.\nGraphical break-even analysis â Break-even point can be found graphically by plotting costs and income against output quantity, where the lines cross indicates when benefits equal costs.","exq.manual-activity-61e25cd8-8548-4af8-a898-1bd559abddb6.0.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-61e25cd8-8548-4af8-a898-1bd559abddb6.0.tries":"1","exq.manual-activity-5bf394d9-7a1c-4084-b567-7058607a1d22.0":"Breakdown into logical parts â A time estimate is created by dividing the work element into smaller, logical parts or tasks to better understand and estimate the time needed for each part.\nDetailed task listing â Listing all tasks in detail helps ensure no unknowns remain and allows for more accurate time estimates for each component of the work.\nInclude all supporting activities â Estimates should include time for project management, planning, meetings, quality assurance, and documentation, not just the main work tasks","exq.manual-activity-5bf394d9-7a1c-4084-b567-7058607a1d22.0.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-5bf394d9-7a1c-4084-b567-7058607a1d22.0.tries":"1","exq.manual-activity-5bf394d9-7a1c-4084-b567-7058607a1d22.1":"Include interface implementation/testing â Time estimates must account for implementing and testing interfaces between components to ensure proper integration where applicable.\nConsider dependencies on other components â Understanding dependencies on other components helps include the time needed to coordinate and test interactions in the estimate.\nAccount for additional complexity â Interfaces add complexity and potential delays, so estimates should reflect the extra effort required to handle these aspects.","exq.manual-activity-5bf394d9-7a1c-4084-b567-7058607a1d22.1.ok":true,"exq.manual-activity-5bf394d9-7a1c-4084-b567-7058607a1d22.1.tries":"1","selfassess-0":true,"selfassess-1":true,"selfassess-2":true,"selfassess-3":true,"selfassess.savedAt":"2026-09-18T11:15:04.963Z"},"exercises":{"manual-activity-61e25cd8-8548-4af8-a898-1bd559abddb6":{"best":8,"last":8,"total":10,"attempts":2},"manual-activity-5bf394d9-7a1c-4084-b567-7058607a1d22":{"best":10,"last":10,"total":12,"attempts":2}}},"114074":{"activities":{"Lesson & Training Aids":true,"Formative Assessment":true},"logbook":{"videoengagement.viewCount.https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/QMpWkkqX1eM":"7","videoengagement.watchSeconds.https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/QMpWkkqX1eM":"324"},"quizzes":{},"exercises":{}},"HWSW2":{"activities":{"Formative Assessment":true,"Lesson & Training Aids":true,"Summative Assessment":true,"POE Evidence":true},"quizzes":{"hwsw2-hardware":{"best":12,"total":15,"attempts":3,"history":[{"score":12,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-17T05:57:13.293Z"},{"score":12,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-17T05:54:32.511Z"},{"score":9,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-17T05:08:03.234Z"}]},"hwsw2-software":{"best":14,"total":15,"attempts":1,"history":[{"score":14,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-17T06:12:22.696Z"}]},"hwsw2-storage":{"best":15,"total":15,"attempts":2,"history":[{"score":15,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-17T17:09:26.299Z"},{"score":11,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-17T17:04:55.623Z"}]},"hwsw2-processing":{"best":15,"total":15,"attempts":2,"history":[{"score":15,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-17T17:19:46.726Z"},{"score":13,"total":15,"date":"2026-08-17T17:16:04.135Z"}]}},"logbook":{"quiz.hwsw2-storage.picks":"{\"0\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"1\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"2\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2]},\"3\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2]},\"4\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"5\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"6\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2]},\"7\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[3]},\"8\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2]},\"9\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"10\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[3]},\"11\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"12\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"13\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"14\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2]}}","quiz.hwsw2-processing.picks":"{\"0\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"1\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"2\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"3\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"4\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"5\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"6\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"7\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"8\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"9\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"10\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"11\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"12\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"13\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"14\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]}}","slidegate.1":true,"slidegate.3":true,"slidegate.4":true,"slidegate.5":true,"slidegate.6":true,"slidegate.7":true},"exercises":{}},"HWSW":{"activities":{"Formative Assessment":true,"Lesson & Training Aids":true,"Summative Assessment":true,"POE Evidence":true},"logbook":{"quiz.hwsw-day1.picks":"{\"0\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"1\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"2\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"3\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0,1]},\"4\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0]},\"5\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"6\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"7\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"8\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"9\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2]},\"10\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"11\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]}}","quiz.hwsw-day2.picks":"{\"0\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"1\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"2\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0,1,3]},\"3\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"4\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"5\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"6\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"7\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"8\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"9\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2]},\"10\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0,1,2]},\"11\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[3]}}","quiz.hwsw-psu.picks":"{\"0\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2]},\"1\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"2\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"3\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"4\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2]},\"5\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[0,1,2]},\"6\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[2]},\"7\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"picks\":[1]},\"8\":{\"kind\":\"order\",\"order\":[0,1,2,3,4,6,5,7]},\"9\":{\"kind\":\"match\",\"picks\":{\"0\":0,\"1\":1,\"2\":2,\"3\":3,\"4\":4}}}","exq.hwsw-identify.0":"RAM is the computer's short-term memory. It stores the programs and data that the CPU is currently using. When many browser tabs and Excel spreadsheets are open, they consume a large amount of RAM.\nIf the PC does not have enough RAM:\nWindows moves some data from RAM to the storage drive (pagefile).\nThe storage drive is much slower than RAM.\nThe disk light flickers constantly because the drive is being accessed all the time.\nThe computer becomes very slow when switching between tabs or applications.\nBy adding more RAM, more data can stay in fast memory instead of being written to and read from the disk. This reduces paging, improves responsiveness, and allows the user to run multiple browser tabs and Excel files smoothly.","exq.hwsw-identify.0.ok":true,"exq.hwsw-identify.1":"HDD (Hard Disk Drive)\nUses spinning magnetic disks (platters) and a moving read/write head to store and retrieve data.\nMechanical parts make it slower and more prone to physical damage.\nUsually provides more storage space for a lower cost.\nAdvantages:\nCheaper per gigabyte.\nAvailable in very large capacities.\nDisadvantages:\nSlower boot and loading times.\nUses more power.\nMore susceptible to failure due to moving parts.\nSSD (Solid State Drive)\nUses flash memory chips to store data.\nHas no moving parts.\nData can be accessed much faster than on an HDD.\nAdvantages:\nMuch faster startup and application loading.\nMore reliable because there are no moving parts.\nUses less power, improving laptop battery life.\nOperates silently.\nDisadvantages:\nMore expensive per gigabyte than an HDD.\nWhich Would I Specify for a New Laptop?\nâ I would specify an SSD, preferably an NVMe SSD.\nWhy?\nThe laptop will boot faster.\nPrograms such as Microsoft Office, web browsers, and business applications will open much quicker.\nThe system will be more responsive when multitasking.\nBetter battery life because SSDs consume less power.\nGreater durability if the laptop is moved or bumped.\nConclusion\nFor a new laptop, an SSD is the best choice because it delivers significantly faster performance, better reliability, lower power consumption, and a better overall user experience than an HDD.","exq.hwsw-identify.1.ok":true,"exq.hwsw-identify.2":"A designer should use a DisplayPort connection (or HDMI 2.1 if available) because it provides the bandwidth needed for 4K resolution at high refresh rates. VGA should not be used because it is an older analog standard that cannot properly support modern 4K high-refresh-rate displays.","exq.hwsw-identify.2.ok":false,"exq.hwsw-identify.3":"Recommended Printer: â Laser Printer (or a departmental laser multifunction printer)\nWhy?\nThe finance department prints about 5,000 pages per month, which is a high print volume. Laser printers are designed for heavy-duty printing and are more cost-effective for large workloads.\nAdvantages of a Laser Printer\nFast printing speeds, ideal for large financial reports.\nLower cost per page compared to most inkjet printers.\nSharp, professional text quality, which is important for business documents.\nToner cartridges last longer than ink cartridges.\nBetter suited for high monthly duty cycles and continuous use.\nWhy Not an Inkjet Printer?\nâ Inkjet printers are generally:\nSlower for large print jobs.\nMore expensive to operate at high volumes.\nMore likely to require frequent cartridge replacements.\nBetter suited to low-volume printing or photo printing.","exq.hwsw-identify.3.ok":true,"exq.hwsw-identify.4":"Device that connects the office network to the Internet: Router\nWhat it does:\nA router connects the office's local network (LAN) to the Internet (WAN).\nIt routes data between internal devices and external networks.\nIt manages Internet traffic and often provides security features such as a firewall.\nDevice that connects all office PCs to each other: Network Switch\nWhat it does:\nA switch connects computers, printers, servers, and other devices within the same office network (LAN).\nIt receives data from one device and sends it only to the intended device.\nThis allows employees to share files, printers, and network resources efficiently.","exq.hwsw-identify.4.ok":true,"exq.hwsw-history-cloud.0":"She wrote the first computer algorithm\n\nIn 1843, Ada Lovelace created a step-by-step set of instructions for the Analytical Engine to calculate Bernoulli numbers. This is considered the world's first computer program\nShe worked a century before modern computers\n\nLovelace's program was written in the 1840s, long before electronic computers appeared in the 20th century\nShe understood that computers could do more than calculations\n\nShe predicted that machines could work with symbols, music, and other types of information, not just numbers.\nShe laid the foundation for modern programming\n\nHer ideas showed how a machine could follow a sequence of instructions to solve problems, which is a core principle of computer programming today.","exq.hwsw-history-cloud.0.ok":true,"exq.hwsw-history-cloud.0.tries":"1","exq.hwsw-history-cloud.1":"A computer was a job title\n\nBefore digital computers, a \"computer\" was someone whose job was to solve mathematical equations and process data manually.\nMany NASA computers were women mathematicians\n\nAt NASA and its predecessor NACA, hundreds of women with mathematics and science backgrounds were hired to perform calculations for aviation and space research.\nThey helped make space missions possible\n\nHuman computers calculated flight paths, analyzed test data, and supported rocket and spacecraft projects that were critical to the early Space Race.\nFamous NASA human computers\n\nNotable women included Katherine Johnson, Dorothy Vaughan, and Mary Jackson, whose work contributed significantly to America's space missions in the 1950s and 1960s.","exq.hwsw-history-cloud.1.ok":true,"exq.hwsw-history-cloud.1.tries":"1","exq.hwsw-history-cloud.2":"ENIAC (1945) was the first general-purpose electronic computer.\nProgramming it meant setting switches and re-plugging cables by hand.\nIt was programmed by six women â the ENIAC Six â without manuals or training, effectively inventing programming as a job.\nIt weighed 30 tons and used about 18,000 vacuum tubes (valves).","exq.hwsw-history-cloud.2.ok":true,"exq.hwsw-history-cloud.2.tries":"2","exq.hwsw-history-cloud.3":"\"The cloud is not a magical place. It is a network of real computers in secure data centers around the world. When you save a file or use an online service, your data is stored and processed on those computers, and you access it over the internet.\"\nData is stored in data centers\n\nCompanies such as Microsoft, Amazon, and Google operate massive buildings filled with computer hardware that store data and run applications.\nYou access it through the internet\n\nWhen you use cloud services such as email, file storage, or online banking, your device connects through the internet to those remote servers, which process and she hardware is managed for you\nthe hardware is managed for you\n\nInstead of buying and maintaining your own servers, the cloud provider owns, secures, powers, cools, and maintains the hardware while users simply access the services they need.","exq.hwsw-history-cloud.3.ok":false,"exq.hwsw-history-cloud.3.tries":"2","exq.hwsw-history-cloud.4":"A rack server â the compute workhorse, stacked in a 42U cabinet (or a blade chassis packing many servers into one enclosure).\nA CRAC/cooling unit removes the heat that thousands of machines generate (hot/cold aisles, chillers).\n.\nA SAN storage array â shelves of drives serving storage to many machines at once.\nPower hardware: a UPS battery system bridges outages instantly, a diesel generator carries long outages, and PDUs distribute power to each rack.\nOther data-centre kit: a KVM console, patch panels and core switches or firewall appliances.","exq.hwsw-history-cloud.4.ok":true,"exq.hwsw-history-cloud.4.tries":"2"},"quizzes":{"hwsw-day1":{"best":11,"total":12,"attempts":4,"history":[{"score":11,"total":12,"date":"2026-08-18T04:30:41.944Z"},{"score":10,"total":12,"date":"2026-08-18T04:29:37.864Z"},{"score":11,"total":12,"date":"2026-08-18T04:28:42.734Z"}]},"hwsw-da